<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=variolation">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">Variolation</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as smallpox inoculation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Variolation was the deliberate infection of a person with material from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> patient, usually rubbed into scratches in the skin or, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, blown up the nose as powdered scabs, to cause a mild illness that gave immunity; its oldest documented use was in 16th-century China. Only 1–2% of those variolated died, against 30% of those who caught smallpox naturally. Practised also in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, it reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> in the 1720s and was replaced by Edward Jenner's safer cowpox vaccine after 1796.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/variolation.jpg" alt="Variolation" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Samuel Freeman / After Godfrey Kneller · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lady_Mary_Wortley_Montagu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>16th century</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>China</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Some sources date it to the 11th century in China, and claims of an ancient Indian origin are disputed.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox-vaccine">Vaccination<span>1796</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=variolation">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
